The code statements that lead to the bug are:
dmac->icu.pdev = of_find_device_by_node(args.np);
if (dmac_index > RZV2H_MAX_DMAC_INDEX) {
    return -EINVAL;
}
Reason:
The call to `of_find_device_by_node()` increments the reference count of the ICU device. If the subsequent check for `dmac_index` fails (or any other error occurs before the device is put), the function returns early without calling `put_device()`, causing a device reference leak. The only release was in the `rz_dmac_remove()` path, which is never reached on probe failure.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
static void rz_dmac_put_device(void *_dev)
{
    put_device(dev);
}
ret = devm_add_action_or_reset(dev, rz_dmac_put_device, &dmac->icu.pdev->dev);
Reason:
The fix registers a devm action that calls `put_device()` on the ICU device when the device is removed or if probe fails. This ensures that the reference taken by `of_find_device_by_node()` is always released, even on probe failure paths. The corresponding manual `platform_device_put(dmac->icu.pdev)` in `rz_dmac_remove()` is removed because the devm action now handles the cleanup.

